{% extends "base.twig" %}

{#
	Article page. Figma `blog / single` (21:148).

	The 2026-09 revision rebuilt the top of this screen and added two blocks to
	the bottom of it:

	  post-hero      a full-bleed band with a photo behind it, the trail above
	                 the title and date · reading time below it. It replaces the
	                 flat `page-hero` the first revision drew here.
	  author-box     new — the byline, derived from the post's WordPress author.
	  related-posts  new — derived from the query, not authored.

	One band of the frame is deliberately not here. `post-visual` is an empty
	`image-placeholder` labelled "תמונת כותרת" — the featured image — drawn as
	its own 1200-wide figure under the hero. In WordPress a post has exactly
	one featured image, and the hero band above already carries it full bleed,
	so building both would print the same photograph twice inside 400px. The
	picture appears once, where the frame's *filled* layer puts it.

	The lead paragraph went with the old header for the same kind of reason:
	the frame has none, and the body's opening paragraph is Body/Large in this
	revision, which is the job the lead was doing. The excerpt still feeds the
	meta description and both index cards.

	The body is the only place in the theme where the editor writes free-form
	HTML, so `.article-body` styles descendants directly rather than expecting
	BEM classes on prose it doesn't control.

	The TOC sits on the end edge (left in RTL) as a secondary column at desktop
	and above the article at mobile, and is built by JS from the rendered h2
	nodes.
#}

{% block content %}

	{#
		The photo behind the header is the post's own featured image.

		An archive takes its hero photo from the options page because a query
		has no row to carry one; a post is not a query — it already owns
		exactly one picture, and every post in the blog has one. A site-wide
		option would put the same photograph behind every article forever,
		which is the opposite of what a revision that exists to add
		photography is asking for.

		`section-background.twig` reads an ACF image array, so a Timber image
		is mapped onto that shape here rather than teaching the partial a
		second input. `scrim: deep` is the frame's own rgba(11,12,14,0.6), and
		`eager` because this band is the page's LCP candidate.
	#}
	{% set hero_background = post.thumbnail ? {
		url: post.thumbnail.src,
		ID: post.thumbnail.id,
		width: post.thumbnail.width,
		height: post.thumbnail.height
	} : null %}

	<section class="post-hero{% if hero_background %} post-hero--background{% endif %}">
		<div class="container post-hero__inner">
			{% include 'partials/ui/breadcrumbs.twig' with {} only %}

			<h1 class="post-hero__title"><bdi>{{ post.title }}</bdi></h1>

			{#
				Date · reading time, below the title — the frame moved it there
				from above it, and swapped the category for the reading
				estimate. The category has not gone anywhere: it is the third
				entry in the trail directly above.

				Reading time is derived from the rendered content. An authored
				field would be wrong the first time someone edits a paragraph.
			#}
			{# `1 דקות` is not Hebrew. One minute is a different noun form, not
			   the same noun with a different number in front of it. #}
			{% set minutes = reading_time(post.id) %}

			<p class="post-hero__meta">
				<time datetime="{{ post.date('c') }}">{{ post.date('d.m.Y') }}</time>
				· {{ minutes == 1 ? 'דקת קריאה' : minutes ~ ' דקות קריאה' }}
			</p>
		</div>

		{# Last child on purpose: reveal.js animates the children of the FIRST child. #}
		{% include 'partials/ui/section-background.twig' with {
			image: hero_background,
			scrim: 'deep',
			eager: true
		} only %}
	</section>

	<article class="article">
		<div class="container article__inner">

			{#
				The aside comes first in the DOM on purpose. Figma stacks the TOC
				*above* the article at 390 and puts it beside it at 1440, and DOM
				order is what a single-column grid follows — so the two-column
				case is the one placed explicitly, in _blocks.scss.

				Doing it the other way round and reordering visually on mobile
				would leave the TOC drawn above the article but tabbed after it.
			#}
			<div class="article__columns">
				<aside class="article__aside">
					{% include 'partials/ui/article-toc.twig' with { post: post } only %}
				</aside>

				<div class="article-body" data-toc-source>
					{{ post.content }}
				</div>
			</div>

		</div>
	</article>

	{% include 'partials/ui/author-card.twig' with { author: post.author } only %}

	{% include 'partials/ui/related-posts.twig' with {
		posts: related_posts(post.id)
	} only %}

	{#
		Kept against the frame, and recorded rather than silent: `blog / single`
		closed with a cta-band in the first revision and the 2026-09 one drops
		it for the author box and the related row. Those two are navigation and
		attribution — neither of them asks for the work. An article is the one
		screen a stranger lands on from a search result, and without this band
		it offers no way to get in touch at all.

		It is opt-in either way: no `article_cta` link on the options page, no
		band.
	#}
	{% if options.article_cta.link.url %}
		{% include 'partials/sections/cta-band.twig' with {
			section: options.article_cta
		} only %}
	{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
